1. **BLOCKING — Nonzero-exit classification can permit a second accepted submission.** [Patch:82–84](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/ops/patches/kaggle-submit-kernel-v2.diff:82) clears pending whenever output lacks a short denylist of error strings. That does **not** establish rejection. If the server accepts but the CLI is killed before printing its reference (`rc=137`, empty output), pending is cleared and neither SHA nor day is recorded. Response-decoding errors can produce the same outcome. Offline probes confirmed that `137/empty`, `JSONDecodeError`, and `HTTPError: 503` all clear pending; the last misses because the regex requires a trailing space. Default every unproven outcome to ambiguous. Clear pending only on positively identified rejection evidence.

2. **BLOCKING — Root `agent.yaml` check accepts filenames containing spaces.** [Patch:36](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/ops/patches/kaggle-submit-kernel-v2.diff:36) parses `unzip -l` using whitespace fields. An archive containing **only `agent.yaml extra`** passes because `$4` becomes `agent.yaml`. I reproduced this offline; the check returned zero without any root `agent.yaml`. This is inherited from HEAD but remains a bypass in the patched result. Inspect exact archive member names, preferably with Python `zipfile`, and require a regular root member named exactly `agent.yaml`.

**NON-BLOCKING**

- Kernel artifact binding remains sound for the supplied deterministic kernel source: [download/hash verification](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/ops/patches/kaggle-submit-kernel-v2.diff:51) and [submission](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/ops/patches/kaggle-submit-kernel-v2.diff:70) use the same pinned version and filename. I found no additional unauthorized-SHA, authorization-grep, or size bypass in that configured path.
